    16	Why not [x, y, vx, vy, theta, omega] (a free planar rigid body)?
    17	A differential-drive rover cannot slide sideways: the wheel contact constraint pins
    18	the BODY-FRAME lateral velocity to (approximately) zero at all times -- it is not a
    19	quantity that varies and needs its own filter state, it is a constraint. Estimating
    20	world-frame [vx, vy] is, after rotating into the body frame, equivalent to estimating
    21	[v_forward, v_lateral] where v_lateral's true value is always ~0. But nothing in the
    22	process model or in the measurements (vision gives position + heading only, never a
    23	velocity) ties v_lateral back to zero: process noise injects independent uncertainty
    24	into vx and vy every step, so the lateral component is a pure random walk with no
    25	corrective term. Its variance grows unboundedly between vision fixes and, because
    26	theta itself is uncertain, that spurious lateral energy rotates into the x/y estimate
    27	-- an unobservable mode contaminating the very quantity we care about. This is exactly
    28	the "redundant, unobservable-lateral-velocity" problem flagged by the lead. The
    29	unicycle state sidesteps it structurally: v and omega are the ONLY velocity states and
    30	the nonholonomic constraint is baked into the kinematics (dx/dt, dy/dt both scale off
    31	the single scalar v), so there is no lateral-velocity component to mis-estimate.
